{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"05","section":"05","chapter_title":"AI-Enhanced Presentation Development","title":"Self-Recording With AI Coaching","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting self-recording with ai coaching to AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat100-supplement-05-05-lo-01","statement":"Interpret AI speech-coaching metrics (pace, filler count, clarity) as gameable proxies for the listener's experience, not as goals"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-05-05-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-05-05-lo-03","statement":"Apply one-change-per-take, single-variable practice and confirm each metric gain with a human listener"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-05-05-lo-04","statement":"Document accepted, modified, and rejected AI coach suggestions in the critique log with reasons"}],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development","cat100_supplement_self_recording_with_ai_coaching","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":5,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/05/04"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_5_5_self_recording_with_ai_coaching_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_5_5_self_recording_with_ai_coaching_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_5_5_self_recording_with_ai_coaching_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_5_5_self_recording_with_ai_coaching_calibration"],"lo_practice_map":{"cat100-supplement-05-05-lo-01":["cat100_supplement_5_5_self_recording_with_ai_coaching_artifact_signal"],"cat100-supplement-05-05-lo-02":["cat100_supplement_5_5_self_recording_with_ai_coaching_annotation_signal"],"cat100-supplement-05-05-lo-03":["cat100_supplement_5_5_self_recording_with_ai_coaching_calibration","cat100_supplement_5_5_self_recording_with_ai_coaching_ai_boundary"],"cat100-supplement-05-05-lo-04":["cat100_supplement_5_5_self_recording_with_ai_coaching_annotation_signal"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Self-Recording With AI Coaching\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Self Recording With AI Coaching, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/05-05-self-recording-with-ai-coaching.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Self-Recording With AI Coaching: Self Recording With AI Coaching, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nYou cannot fix delivery you cannot see. Speakers routinely have no idea they talk at 190 words per minute or say \"um\" sixteen times in two minutes, because in the moment those habits feel normal. Recording yourself and running an AI speech coach turns invisible habits into numbers: pace in words per minute, filler-word counts, pause length, even a rough clarity score. Those numbers make practice targeted instead of vague.\n\nThe trap is treating the score as the goal. A coach metric is a proxy for what a human listener experiences, and proxies can be gamed. This section teaches you to use AI coaching feedback to drive one measurable change per take and then confirm with a human that the change was real. The artifact is a critique log showing the flag, the single fix, the new numbers, and the human check.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Interpret AI speech-coaching metrics (pace, filler count, clarity) as gameable proxies for the listener's experience, not as goals.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Apply one-change-per-take, single-variable practice and confirm each metric gain with a human listener.\n- Document accepted, modified, and rejected AI coach suggestions in the critique log with reasons.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student records a practice take and the coach flags three problems: rushed pace, sixteen fillers, and an unclear opening. Overwhelmed, he tries to fix all three at once in the next take, and the recording falls apart because he is monitoring too many things to actually speak. A second student fixes one thing, the rushed opening, re-records only that section, and watches her clarity score climb while she keeps everything else stable. Single-variable practice beats trying to fix everything, because you can tell what actually worked.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nAI speech coaching gives you **process metrics**: measurements of how you delivered, not whether the audience understood. Pace (words per minute), filler frequency, pause distribution, and clarity estimates are all proxies. They are useful because they are concrete and repeatable, and they are limited because a number can improve while the talk gets worse. You can hit a perfect 140 wpm by speaking in a flat monotone, which the coach rewards and a human finds unbearable. (The numbers themselves are well-grounded: Microsoft's Speaker Coach guidance puts a comfortable English presentation rate at roughly 100-165 wpm, so 140 sits mid-range and 190 reads as rushed, and speech research shows listening comprehension declines as word rate climbs well past conversational speed; Foulke, 1968; Griffiths, 1992.)\n\nTwo disciplines keep coaching honest:\n\n- **One change per take.** Isolate a single variable, re-record, and compare the same metric before and after. If you change three things at once and the score moves, you cannot attribute the gain, and you cannot reproduce it.\n- **Human-in-the-loop confirmation.** A metric improvement counts only when a person confirms the talk is actually clearer. The coach measures the proxy; a peer measures the thing the proxy stands for.\n\nTreat a flag as a hypothesis, not a verdict. \"Pace: 190 wpm (rushed)\" is the coach saying the audience may struggle to keep up. Your job is to test whether slowing the opening makes a listener report it as clearer, not to chase the number for its own sake. The right pause that lets a key figure land is good delivery even if it nudges the average pace; deleting it to hit a target would be optimizing the proxy against the goal.\n\nTools that do this in 2026: Microsoft PowerPoint Speaker Coach reports pace, filler words, monotone pitch, pronunciation, and originality, plus a Rehearsal Report with a pace graph and filler counts; Yoodli tracks filler words, speaking pace, word choice, and eye contact, with real-time nudges and progress tracking; Poised reports filler words, pace, clarity, and confidence in real time during live video calls, plus a post-meeting recap and an overall Poised Score. Exact metric names, and whether a numeric \"clarity score\" even exists, vary by tool (PowerPoint reports pace, pitch, and fillers but no single clarity number, while Poised surfaces a clarity metric), and model and UI churn means the labels you see may differ.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nGoal: turn one round of AI coaching feedback into a measurable delivery improvement.\n\nTake 1, AI coach report:\n\n```\nPace:        190 wpm  (flag: rushed)\nFiller words: 16      (\"um\" x9, \"like\" x7)\nClarity:     2/5      (flag: opening unclear)\n```\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Pick one issue to fix this take: the rushed opening.\n2. Re-record only the first 30 seconds, aiming near 140 wpm, replacing fillers with short pauses but keeping the pause that lets the key number land.\n3. Re-run the coach on Take 2:\n\n   ```\n   Pace:        144 wpm\n   Filler words: 4\n   Clarity:     4/5\n   ```\n\n4. Confirm with a human: a peer says the open is now clear, so the change is real, not just a better metric.\n\nNotice that the single fix moved more than one metric, because rushing and fillers were related symptoms of the same problem. That is fine; what matters is that you changed one *behavior* (slowing the opening), so you can credit the gain to it. The human check in step 4 is what separates a real improvement from a number you nudged.\n\nWork one coaching round at a time, predicting each move before you reveal it:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Turn a coaching report into one attributable improvement\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The coach flags three problems at once: rushed pace, sixteen fillers, and an unclear opening. What do you do in the next take?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Fix all three at once to save time\",\"Pick one behavior to change, the rushed opening, and hold the rest constant\",\"Ignore the flags and re-record the whole talk\",\"Lower the coach’s sensitivity so fewer flags appear\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Change one behavior. Fixing three things at once means you cannot attribute any gain or reproduce it. Isolate the rushed opening and re-record only that section.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Take 2 shows clarity rising from 2/5 to 4/5. Is the improvement confirmed?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Yes; the higher score proves it is better\",\"Not yet; a human must confirm the opening actually lands\",\"Yes, as long as pace also dropped\",\"No; the score must hit 5/5 first\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Not yet. The score is a proxy. A peer must confirm the opening is genuinely clearer before you trust the gain, because a monotone delivery can game the metric.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Log the flag, the single behavior you changed, the before and after numbers, and the human confirmation. Note that you kept the deliberate pause before the key figure rather than chasing a lower average pace.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A student raises his AI coach clarity score by speaking in a flat monotone at a steady 140 wpm. Why is the higher score not proof of a better talk?\" explanation=\"Coach metrics are proxies for the audience experience, and proxies can be gamed. A monotone can ace the pace metric while a human listener finds it unbearable, which is why human confirmation is required.\" options='[{\"text\":\"A higher coach score always means a better talk\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The metric is a proxy that can be gamed; only a human confirms the talk improved\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"140 wpm is too slow to score well\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The coach cannot measure pace accurately\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nUse coaching metrics to target one behavioral change per take, then confirm with a human before you trust the gain. A better score that no listener can feel is not an improvement.\n\nCheck a coaching round against three questions:\n\n1. **Purpose check:** Which delivery problem most hurt the audience's understanding, and did you target it?\n2. **Evidence check:** What were the before and after numbers for the metric you targeted, from the same coach on comparable takes?\n3. **Revision check:** Which single behavior did you change, and what did a human listener say about the result?\n\nIf a score improved but no person confirmed the difference, treat the gain as unverified.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is that a higher coach score equals a better talk. The score is a proxy; a monotone delivery can ace the pace metric while putting the audience to sleep. Optimize the listener's experience, and let the metric follow.\n\nThe second is fixing everything in one take. When you change pace, fillers, and structure simultaneously, you cannot attribute any improvement, and the next take has no reproducible lesson. One variable, one take, one comparison.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply single-variable practice to a different flag. Suppose Take 1 is well-paced but the coach reports long dead-air pauses averaging four seconds where you lose your place. The temptation is to also rewrite your script and change your tone, but you hold those constant and fix only the pauses, perhaps by adding short cue notes so you never go blank. Re-record, and the pause metric drops while pace and fillers stay roughly where they were, which tells you the cue notes, not luck, did the work.\n\nThen confirm with a human, because the metric alone can mislead. A peer might say the pauses are gone but you now sound rushed through the cue points, a side effect the coach scored as \"improved.\" That feedback sends you to the next single change: keep the cues but restore one deliberate pause before the key result. Each take isolates a variable, and the human check catches what the proxy misses.\n\nOrder the single-variable coaching loop so each step depends on the one before it:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Record a take and run the AI coach to get baseline metrics\", \"Pick the one flag that most hurts the audience, not just the score\", \"Change a single behavior and re-record only that section\", \"Re-run the same coach and compare the before and after numbers\", \"Ask a human whether the change actually made the talk clearer\", \"Log the flag, the one change, the numbers, and the human confirmation\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: on the left, each coach flag; on the right, the single change you would make and how you would confirm it with a person. Flag any metric you might be tempted to game. By the end of 05.05, your note should show targeted, attributable practice rather than score-chasing.\n\nA strong reader of this section can answer four questions:\n\n1. Which delivery flag most affects the audience, not just the score?\n2. What single behavior would you change to address it?\n3. What before and after numbers would show the change worked?\n4. How would a human confirm the gain is real?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe critique log is strong when a reviewer can see the flag, the one behavior you changed, the before and after numbers, and a human confirmation. Treat a clean second-take report as incomplete until a person vouches for it.\n\nA useful entry would sound like this: \"The coach flagged a rushed, unclear opening at 190 wpm. I re-recorded only the first 30 seconds at about 144 wpm and replaced fillers with short pauses, keeping the pause before the key number. Clarity rose from 2/5 to 4/5, and a peer confirmed the opening now lands. I rejected the coach's suggestion to remove all pauses, because the deliberate pause helps the key figure register.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nTake the same skill to a setting with no AI coach: a friend listens to your practice run and gives notes. The single-variable discipline transfers intact. You ask the friend to watch for one thing per run (pace, then fillers, then the opening), change one behavior between runs, and treat their reaction as the confirmation the coach used to provide. The metric source changed from software to a person; the habit of isolating one change and verifying it did not.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nConnect the ideas this section builds on. See how single-variable coaching with a human check feeds evidence-based revision of the delivery log:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_self_recording_with_ai_coaching\", \"cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/05-05-self-recording-with-ai-coaching-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"05.05\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation of process metrics as proxies and why human confirmation matters.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a take that gamed the pace metric with one a listener actually found clearer.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the one-change-per-take rule to a different coaching flag.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where a human check disagreed with a metric.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions for CAT 100 05.05. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_self_recording_with_ai_coaching\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm you changed one behavior per take, recorded before and after numbers for the targeted metric, and got a human to confirm the improvement was real.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- Foulke, E. (1968). Listening comprehension as a function of word rate. Journal of Communication. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1460-2466.1968.tb00070.x\n- Griffiths, R. (1992). Speech rate and listening comprehension: Further evidence of the relationship. TESOL Quarterly. https://doi.org/10.2307/3587015\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_5_self_recording_with_ai_coaching_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_self_recording_with_ai_coaching","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_self_recording_with_ai_coaching","cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development"],"stem":"An AI coach reports your practice take had 16 filler words, a 190 wpm pace, and a 'rushed' clarity flag. Which response shows you used the feedback well?","options":["Submit the take because it was your first try.","Re-record the rushed section at about 140 wpm, replacing filler with brief pauses, and keep the pauses that help comprehension.","Delete all pauses to push the pace even higher.","Ignore the report and re-record nothing."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"You act on the specific flags, slow the rushed part, and keep meaningful pauses rather than blindly chasing one number.","difficulty":"easy","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_5_5_self_recording_with_ai_coaching_metric_chasing","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-05-05-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_5_self_recording_with_ai_coaching_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"Which note gives BigAL a usable self-recording problem?","options":["My recording is ok.","The AI coach flags my open with 9 filler words, but cutting them all made me sound robotic. How do I reduce fillers without losing natural pacing?","Help with my video.","I recorded it."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The note names a concrete tension (filler reduction versus natural pacing) so feedback can be specific.","difficulty":"medium","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_5_5_self_recording_with_ai_coaching_one_take","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-05-05-lo-02","cat100-supplement-05-05-lo-04"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_5_self_recording_with_ai_coaching_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"stem":"What is a sound boundary for AI coaching on your own recording?","options":["Treat the AI clarity score as the final grade on your delivery.","Use the AI flags to pick one issue to fix per take, then confirm the fix with your own playback or a peer.","Re-record until the AI score is perfect even if the talk no longer makes sense.","Avoid recording yourself because the AI might be wrong."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The AI provides targeted signals; you verify improvements through playback and human judgment.","difficulty":"hard","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_5_5_self_recording_with_ai_coaching_coach_as_judge","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-05-05-lo-03"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_5_self_recording_with_ai_coaching_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"Which record of your practice session is most useful for later support?","options":["Only the final video file.","The AI flags per take, which one issue you fixed each time, and whether a peer found the result clearer.","Only the total recording time.","Only the number of takes."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Logging the flag, the fix, and the human check turns practice into inspectable improvement evidence.","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-05-05-lo-03"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_5_self_recording_with_ai_coaching_metric_chasing","pattern":"optimizing_only_metrics","description":"Chasing AI-reported metrics (pace, filler count) while ignoring whether the message lands","trigger":"learner cuts every pause to hit a words-per-minute target and the talk becomes rushed and unclear","feedback":"Use metrics as cues, not goals. A pause that aids understanding beats a perfect pace number.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_5_self_recording_with_ai_coaching_one_take","pattern":"first_take_is_final","description":"Treating the first recording as final because re-recording feels tedious","trigger":"learner submits take one despite an AI flag of 14 filler words and a missed key point","feedback":"Plan at least two takes: review the AI feedback, fix one specific issue, then re-record that section.","rail_action":"practice"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_5_self_recording_with_ai_coaching_coach_as_judge","pattern":"ai_coach_as_final_judge","description":"Accepting the AI coaching score as the final verdict on the delivery","trigger":"learner trusts a high clarity score even though a human peer was confused","feedback":"The AI score is one signal. Check it against a human listener and your own playback before trusting it.","rail_action":"explain"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"d15e7d8af9b121b57f70b4797b822a53a356b7193e7087309304421ccbd698ab","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}